# Deploy {{.Environment}}

Do the steps in order. Run commands from the repo root.

Before you start: install `terraform`, `gh` and the `aws` CLI. Log in to both (`gh auth login`, `aws configure`).
{{- if .IsSmall}}

## 1. Fill in the settings

Open `deployments/{{.Environment}}/terraform/terraform.tfvars`. Replace every `CHANGE_ME` and `OWNER/REPO`.

- `ssh_allowed_cidr`: your IP with `/32` at the end. Get your IP with `curl -4 ifconfig.me`. Only this IP can SSH in.
- `github_runner_token`: get it with the command below. **It stops working after 1 hour.** Get it right before step 2.

```sh
gh api -X POST repos/OWNER/REPO/actions/runners/registration-token --jq .token
```

Git ignores this file. Do not commit it.

## 2. Create the server

```sh
cd deployments/{{.Environment}}/terraform
terraform init
terraform apply
```

On first start, the server runs `terraform/runner-init.sh`. It installs Docker and a GitHub runner. Wait a few minutes. Then check the runner is there: repo → Settings → Actions → Runners.

Runner not there? The token probably expired. Get a new token, put it in `terraform.tfvars`, and rebuild the server:

```sh
terraform apply -replace=aws_instance.main
```

`runner-init.sh` runs only once. Editing it later does not change the server.

**Do not lose `terraform.tfstate`.** Terraform needs it to change or delete what it made. Git ignores it. Back it up, or move it to S3: see the `backend "s3"` block at the top of `main.tf`.

## 3. Set up the server

SSH in and become root:

```sh
ssh ubuntu@$(terraform output -raw public_ip)
sudo -i
```

The server needs a key to read your repo. Make one and print it:

```sh
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -N "" -f ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
```

Add it on GitHub: repo → Settings → Deploy keys → Add. Leave "write access" off. Then clone:

```sh
git clone git@github.com:OWNER/REPO.git /root/{{.Name}}
```

Create `/root/{{.Name}}/deployments/{{.Environment}}/.env` with this, and replace every `CHANGE_ME`:

```sh
ENVIRONMENT={{.Environment}}
ADDRESS=:4000
{{- if .HasPostgres}}
POSTGRES_USER=app
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME
POSTGRES_DB={{.Name}}
GOOSE_DBSTRING=postgres://app:CHANGE_ME@postgres:5432/{{.Name}}?sslmode=disable
{{- end}}
{{- if .HasRedis}}
REDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379/0
{{- end}}
{{- if .IsGrpc}}
DOMAIN=api.example.com
{{- else}}
TUNNEL_TOKEN=CHANGE_ME
{{- end}}
```
{{- if .HasPostgres}}

Use the same password in `POSTGRES_PASSWORD` and `GOOSE_DBSTRING`.
{{- end}}
{{- if .IsGrpc}}

## 4. Domain

Add an A record for your `DOMAIN` that points to the server IP (`terraform output public_ip`). If your DNS is on Cloudflare, turn the proxy off (grey cloud).

On first start, Caddy gets an HTTPS certificate by itself. Clients connect to `DOMAIN:443`.
{{- else}}

## 4. Domain

Your domain's DNS must be on Cloudflare.

1. Cloudflare → Zero Trust → Networks → Tunnels → create a tunnel.
2. Copy the tunnel token into `TUNNEL_TOKEN` in `.env`.
3. Add a public hostname, for example `api.example.com`, with service `http://{{.Name}}:4000`.

Cloudflare serves HTTPS for you. No ports are open on the server.
{{- end}}

## 5. Deploy

GitHub → Actions → Deploy {{.Environment}} → Run workflow.

The workflow runs on your server. It pulls the code, builds it, {{if .HasPostgres}}runs the database migration, {{end}}and restarts the app.

**It always deploys the `main` branch**, even if you pick another branch in GitHub.

## Add your own environment variables

Add them to `/root/{{.Name}}/deployments/{{.Environment}}/.env` on the server. Then run the Deploy workflow again.

Put secrets (passwords, API keys) there too. Git ignores `.env`, so they stay on the server.
{{- else}}

## 1. Once per AWS account

Skip this if another environment in this AWS account already did it.

```sh
aws iam create-open-id-connect-provider \
  --url https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com \
  --client-id-list sts.amazonaws.com
```

This lets GitHub log in to AWS without a password. If it says "already exists", that is fine.

Then create an HTTPS certificate for your domain in AWS Certificate Manager (ACM). Create it in the region you will use. Wait until its status is "Issued".

## 2. Fill in the settings

Open `deployments/{{.Environment}}/terraform/terraform.tfvars`. Replace every `CHANGE_ME`, `OWNER/REPO` and `ACCOUNT_ID`.

- `region`: the same region as the certificate.
- `certificate_arn`: the certificate from step 1.

Git ignores this file. Do not commit it. Later it will hold your secrets.

## 3. Create the cloud resources

```sh
cd deployments/{{.Environment}}/terraform
terraform init
terraform apply
```

This takes a while. The app runs a placeholder image until your first deploy.
{{- if .IsGrpc}} The placeholder fails the gRPC health check, so ECS restarts it again and again. This is normal. It stops after the first deploy.{{end}}

Point your domain to the load balancer with a CNAME record:

```sh
terraform output load_balancer_dns_name
```

**Do not lose `terraform.tfstate`.** Terraform needs it to change or delete what it made. It also holds your passwords, so keep it private. Git ignores it. Before anyone else runs Terraform, move it to S3: see the `backend "s3"` block at the top of `main.tf`.

## 4. Connect GitHub

Still in `deployments/{{.Environment}}/terraform`. Replace `OWNER/REPO`, then run:

```sh
gh api -X PUT repos/OWNER/REPO/environments/{{.Environment}}
for v in aws_role_arn aws_region aws_private_subnets aws_app_security_group; do
  gh variable set "$(echo "$v" | tr a-z A-Z)" --env {{.Environment}} --body "$(terraform output -raw "$v")"
done
```

This makes a GitHub environment called `{{.Environment}}` and saves the Terraform outputs in it. They are not secret. AWS only accepts deploys from this environment of this repo.

## 5. Deploy

GitHub → Actions → Deploy {{.Environment}} → Run workflow.

The workflow runs `deployments/{{.Environment}}/deploy.sh`. It builds the image, {{if .HasPostgres}}runs the database migration, {{end}}then starts the new version. If the new version is not healthy, AWS goes back to the old one and the workflow fails.

To deploy from your computer instead, log in to AWS and install Docker and `jq`. **Commit your changes first**: the image is named after your last commit. Then:

```sh
cd deployments/{{.Environment}}/terraform
export AWS_REGION=$(terraform output -raw aws_region)
{{- if .HasPostgres}}
export AWS_PRIVATE_SUBNETS=$(terraform output -raw aws_private_subnets)
export AWS_APP_SECURITY_GROUP=$(terraform output -raw aws_app_security_group)
{{- end}}
cd -
bash deployments/{{.Environment}}/deploy.sh
```

## Add your own environment variables

All changes go in `deployments/{{.Environment}}/terraform/main.tf`.

### Normal values

Add a line to `app_environment` in the `locals` block:

```hcl
    { name = "LOG_LEVEL", value = "info" },
```

### Secret values (passwords, API keys)

**Never write a secret value in `main.tf`.** Git tracks that file. Put the value in `terraform.tfvars`. Git ignores that file.

The example adds `JWT_SECRET`. Change `jwt` and `JWT_SECRET` to your own names.

1. Add the value to `terraform.tfvars`:

   ```hcl
   jwt_secret = "the-real-value"
   ```

2. Add this to `main.tf`:

   ```hcl
   variable "jwt_secret" {
     type      = string
     sensitive = true
   }

   resource "aws_secretsmanager_secret" "jwt" {
     name_prefix = "${local.name}-jwt-"
   }

   resource "aws_secretsmanager_secret_version" "jwt" {
     secret_id     = aws_secretsmanager_secret.jwt.id
     secret_string = var.jwt_secret
   }
   ```

3. Add a line to `app_secrets` in the `locals` block:

   ```hcl
       { name = "JWT_SECRET", valueFrom = aws_secretsmanager_secret.jwt.arn },
   ```

4. Allow the app to read the secret. **If you skip this, the app will not start.**
{{if .HasPostgres}}
   Find `resource "aws_iam_role_policy" "execution_secrets"`. Change its `Resource` line to a list:

   ```hcl
         Resource = [
           aws_secretsmanager_secret.database_url.arn,
           aws_secretsmanager_secret.jwt.arn,
         ]
   ```
{{- else}}
   Add this to `main.tf`. For more secrets later, add each one to the `Resource` list.

   ```hcl
   resource "aws_iam_role_policy" "execution_secrets" {
     name = "secrets"
     role = aws_iam_role.execution.id

     policy = jsonencode({
       Version = "2012-10-17"
       Statement = [{
         Effect   = "Allow"
         Action   = "secretsmanager:GetSecretValue"
         Resource = [aws_secretsmanager_secret.jwt.arn]
       }]
     })
   }
   ```
{{- end}}

### Make the change live

```sh
cd deployments/{{.Environment}}/terraform
terraform apply
```

Then run the Deploy workflow again. **`terraform apply` alone does not change the running app.** The new values load only when a deploy starts new containers.
{{- end}}
{{- if .HasPostgres}}

## Database migrations

Each deploy runs `build/migrate.sh` (`goose up`) before it starts the new app. If a migration fails, it is undone, and the old app keeps running.

Rules for every migration:

- Write a working `-- +goose Down`.
- Only add things. Do not drop or rename columns the running app still uses.
- Be careful with `-- +goose NO TRANSACTION` (for example `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY`). It can fail halfway. A failed concurrent index stays behind as an invalid index, and you must drop it by hand.
{{- end}}
